<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_table">Table 1: </span>Statistics of four city datasets.</figcaption>
</figure>
<div id="Sx5.SSx1.SSSx1.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Sx5.SSx1.SSSx1.p2.1" class="ltx_p">To evaluate recommendation performance, we adopt two widely utilized metrics: Hit Rate at a cutoff top K (HR@K) and Normalized discounted cumulative gain at a cutoff top K (N@K). K is selected from <math id="Sx5.SSx1.SSSx1.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\left\{1,5,10\right\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo>{</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>5</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>10</mn><mo>}</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\left\{1,5,10\right\}</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="Sx5.SSx1.SSSx2" class="ltx_subsubsection">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsubsection">Baselines</h4>

<div id="Sx5.SSx1.SSSx2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Sx5.SSx1.SSSx2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We compare <span id="Sx5.SSx1.SSSx2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">KGTB</span> with nine baseline methods for next POI recommendation, which can be categorized into four groups. (1) <span id="Sx5.SSx1.SSSx2.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">RNN-based method</span>: STLSTM <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib9" title="" class="ltx_ref">Kong and Wu 2018</a>)</cite> redefines LSTM’s gate mechanism with temporal and spatial information. (2) <span id="Sx5.SSx1.SSSx2.p1.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Attention-based method</span>: SASRec <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib25" title="" class="ltx_ref">Zhang et al. 2018</a>)</cite> utilizes the self-attention network. (3) <span id="Sx5.SSx1.SSSx2.p1.1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Graph-based methods</span>: GETNext <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib23" title="" class="ltx_ref">Yang, Liu, and Zhao 2022</a>)</cite> captures global collaborative signals from a global graph that aggregates all users’ trajectories. HMTGRN <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib13" title="" class="ltx_ref">Lim et al. 2022</a>)</cite> constructs spatio-temporal graphs to learn POI-POI relationships. Graph-FlashBack <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib15" title="" class="ltx_ref">Rao et al. 2022</a>)</cite> and ARGAN <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib20" title="" class="ltx_ref">Wang et al. 2023b</a>)</cite> enhance representations via adaptively refined POI graphs. (4) <span id="Sx5.SSx1.SSSx2.p1.1.5" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Generative methods</span>: P5 <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib6" title="" class="ltx_ref">Geng et al. 2022</a>)</cite> unifies recommendation tasks in a shared sequence-to-sequence format. LLM4POI <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib11" title="" class="ltx_ref">Li et al. 2024</a>)</cite> fine-tunes LLMs for next POI recommendation with trajectory prompting. GNPR-SID <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib18" title="" class="ltx_ref">Wang et al. 2025</a>)</cite> tokenizes POIs into spatio-temporal-aware SIDs for a more efficient LLM fine-tuning.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="Sx5.SSx1.SSSx3" class="ltx_subsubsection">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsubsection">Implementation Details</h4>

<div id="Sx5.SSx1.SSSx3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Sx5.SSx1.SSSx3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">For KG construction, the <span id="Sx5.SSx1.SSSx3.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">distance threshold <math id="Sx5.SSx1.SSSx3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">d</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d</annotation></semantics></math></span> is set to 0.2. For the KG-based tokenizer, the layer number for RGCN and quantization is set to 3. For the <math id="Sx5.SSx1.SSSx3.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="l" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>l</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">l</annotation></semantics></math>-th quantization layer, <math id="Sx5.SSx1.SSSx3.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="l\in[1,2,3]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>l</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>2</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">l\in[1,2,3]</annotation></semantics></math>, the codebook size is set to <math id="Sx5.SSx1.SSSx3.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="8^{4(l-1)}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mn>8</mn><mrow><mn>4</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>l</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">8^{4(l-1)}</annotation></semantics></math> for POI and user entities, <math id="Sx5.SSx1.SSSx3.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="8^{2(l-1)}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mn>8</mn><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>l</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">8^{2(l-1)}</annotation></semantics></math> for region and category entities, with each code of 64 dimensions. The balance factor <math id="Sx5.SSx1.SSSx3.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\beta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>β</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\beta</annotation></semantics></math> in <math id="Sx5.SSx1.SSSx3.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{L}_{RQ}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mrow><mi>R</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>Q</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{L}_{RQ}</annotation></semantics></math> is set to 0.25. For multi-behavior learning, we choose a small decoder-only model GPT-2 as our backbone LLM to meet the low latency requirement in recommender systems. The fine-tune process is conducted on an NVIDIA L40 GPU for 8 epochs, using LoRA <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib8" title="" class="ltx_ref">Hu et al. 2022</a>)</cite> with a rank of 16, a learning rate of 0.0002, a batch size of 4. We repeat the experiment 3 times.</p>
</div>
